## Migration: `TimedExecutor` → .NET 8

**Pattern identified: Timeout enforcement** — aborting a thread that exceeds a time limit.

### Why this breaks on .NET 6+

`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` — the runtime no longer supports forcible thread termination. `Thread.ResetAbort()` and `ThreadAbortException` are also gone.

### Migrated code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        try
        {
            var task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);
            // Task.WaitAsync throws OperationCanceledException when the
            // token fires, even if the delegate ignores cancellation.
            return task.WaitAsync(cts.Token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }
}
```

If you can change callers to `async`, prefer the async version:

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public async Task<T> ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync<T>(
        Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        try
        {
            var task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);
            return await task.WaitAsync(cts.Token);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }
}
```

### Key changes

| Before (.NET Framework) | After (.NET 8) |
|---|---|
| `new Thread(...)` + `thread.Start()` | `Task.Run(work, token)` |
| `thread.Join(timeout)` | `task.WaitAsync(token)` |
| `thread.Abort()` | `cts.Cancel()` (implicit via timeout) |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Removed — not needed |

### ⚠️ Critical pitfall: cooperative cancellation

The original code **forcibly killed** the thread. The new code **requests** cancellation — but `Func<T> work` has no `CancellationToken` to check. This means:

- `WaitAsync` will throw after the timeout, but **the delegate keeps running on the thread pool** until it finishes naturally.
- This is safe for short/bounded work, but a CPU-bound infinite loop will leak a thread.

**If the work must actually stop**, pass a token into the work:

```csharp
public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(
    Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
{
    using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
    try
    {
        var task = Task.Run(() => work(cts.Token), cts.Token);
        return task.WaitAsync(cts.Token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
    }
    catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        throw new TimeoutException(
            $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
    }
}
```

Then the caller checks the token at regular intervals:

```csharp
executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(ct =>
{
    foreach (var item in items)
    {
        ct.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // cooperative checkpoint
        Process(item);
    }
    return result;
}, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30));
```

### If the work is truly uncooperative

If you cannot modify the work delegate (e.g., third-party library, native call), **there is no in-process equivalent to `Thread.Abort`**. Move the work to a child process and use `Process.Kill()` on timeout. This is the only reliable way to forcibly stop uncooperative code in modern .NET.
